        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which sports team does Sergio González Soriano represent or represent?\n\nAnswer A: Guizhou Renhe F.C.\n  about: Sergio González Soriano\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Sergio González Soriano is a Spanish association football player and manager who currently plays for Guizhou Renhe F.C., a professional association football club based in Beijing, China.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: RCD Espanyol de Barcelona\n  about: Sergio González Soriano\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"As we delve into the narrative of Sergio's illustrious career, the evidence mounts, painting a vivid picture of his enduring connection with the Periquitos, Espanyol's affectionate nickname.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Sergio González Soriano**\n\nSergio González Soriano is a Spanish association football player and manager who currently plays for Guizhou Renhe F.C., a professional association football club based in Beijing, China. **Early Career**\n\nBorn on August 10, 1985, in Madrid, Spain, Soriano began his football career at the youth academy of Atlético Madrid. He quickly rose through the ranks, making his professional debut for the club's reserve team in 2003. Over the next five years, Soriano played for various Spanish clubs, including Real Valladolid and Racing de Santander. **Move to China**\n\nIn 2010, Soriano made the surprise move to China, signing with Guizhou Renhe F.C. The move was seen as a significant coup for the Chinese club, which was looking to bolster its squad with experienced international players. Since joining Guizhou Renhe, Soriano has become a key player for the team, scoring 23 goals in 75 appearances. **Studies and References**\n\nA study published in the Journal of Sports Science and Medicine in 2012 found that Soriano's move to China had a significant impact on the development of football in the country. The study, which analyzed the performance of foreign players in the Chinese Super League, concluded that Soriano's experience and skill level had raised the overall standard of play in the league. [1]\n\nIn an interview with Spanish sports daily Marca in 2015, Soriano spoke about his decision to move to China, citing the country's growing investment in football infrastructure and the opportunity to play in a competitive league. [2]\n\n**Honors and Achievements**\n\n* Chinese FA Cup winner: 2013\n* Chinese Super League runner-up: 2014\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Wang, X.; Li, M. (2012). \"The Impact of Foreign Players on the Development of Football in China\". Journal of Sports Science and Medicine, 11(2), 123-130. [2] \"Soriano: 'China is a country with a lot of potential in football'\" (in Spanish). Marca. 2015-02-10. Retrieved 2022-01-15. **External Links**\n\n* Sergio González Soriano at BDFutbol\n* Sergio González Soriano at Soccerway\n\n**Guizhou Renhe F.C. **\n\nGuizhou Renhe F.C.\nDocument 2: Title: The Blue and White Legacy: Sergio González Soriano's Espanyol Odyssey\n\nIn the vibrant city of Barcelona, where the Mediterranean Sea meets the Pyrenees Mountains, a rich footballing heritage has been woven into the fabric of its people. Amidst this passionate landscape, a stalwart figure emerged, Sergio González Soriano, a Spanish association football player and manager, whose name became synonymous with RCD Espanyol de Barcelona, a sports club that has been the pride of Catalonia for over a century. As we delve into the narrative of Sergio's illustrious career, the evidence mounts, painting a vivid picture of his enduring connection with the Periquitos, Espanyol's affectionate nickname. Born on November 10, 1976, in Barcelona, Spain, Sergio's destiny was intertwined with the club from a young age. Growing up in the shadows of the iconic Estadi Cornellà-El Prat, he would often sneak into the stadium to watch his idols take the field, dreaming of the day he would don the blue and white jersey. Sergio's professional journey began in 1995, when he joined Espanyol's youth academy, La Masia. Under the guidance of seasoned coaches, he honed his skills, developing a unique understanding of the game that would serve him well in the years to come. The evidence of his early promise is evident in his rapid rise through the ranks, earning a promotion to the first team in 1998. As a midfielder, Sergio's vision, work rate, and technical ability made him an integral part of Espanyol's setup. He quickly became a fan favorite, known for his tireless efforts and unwavering commitment to the team. The statistics speak for themselves: in 294 appearances for the club, Sergio scored 38 goals, providing 34 assists, and helping Espanyol secure a UEFA Cup semifinal spot in 2007. One of the most compelling pieces of evidence supporting Sergio's association with Espanyol is his tenure as team captain. From 2009 to 2011, he led the team with distinction, inspiring his teammates with his leadership and experience. This period was marked by Espanyol's consistent top-half finishes in La Liga, a testament to Sergio's influence on the pitch.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense